# peek-trait

Peek ahead on iterators, using zero-cost abstractions where possible.

`std::iter::Peekable` can only look one item ahead from the front. In addition, it incurs some overhead, as it stores the peeked element in the adapter. This crate adds traits for peeking to arbitrary depth, as well as from the back of double-ended iterators. It also provides some adapters to make iteraters peekable.

## Example

```rust
use peek_trait::{Peek, PeekNth};

let mut it = 0..5;
assert_eq!(it.peek_nth(2), Some(2));
assert_eq!(it.next(), Some(0));

let mut it = vec!["a".to_string(), "b".to_string()].into_iter();
if let Some(first) = it.peek() {
    first.push('!');
}
assert_eq!(it.next().as_deref(), Some("a!"));
```

## Panics

`peek_nth(n)` and `peek_nth_back(n)` on the buffering wrappers panic when `n == usize::MAX`, since
that index can never be buffered.
